{"args":{"goal":"Step 0, before anything else: copy the seeded workspace into your current working\ndirectory, then work there.\n\n    cp -a <RUNS>/seed-copy/. .\n\nDo not modify anything under that source path; copy from it and leave it untouched.\n\nImplement SPEC.md completely.\n\n`SPEC.md` is a specification for `tabula`, a dependency-free tabular-data toolkit: 48 numbered\nrequirements (R-01 .. R-48) grouped into 8 sections, one section per module. The package skeleton\nin `tabula/` already declares every public name the spec promises, and every one of them raises\n`NotImplementedError`. Replace them with real, working code, using the Python 3.11 standard\nlibrary and nothing else.\n\nWrite your own tests beside the seeded `tests/test_contract.py`, and leave `pytest tests/ -q`\ngreen when you stop. `test_contract.py` only checks that the public names exist with the stated\nsignatures - it passes against the untouched stubs, so a green run of it alone proves nothing.\nThe tests that show your code works are the ones you write.\n\nYou are scored by a hidden acceptance suite you will never see, built from the requirement ids in\nSPEC.md and calling only the public API named there. Every stated behaviour is fair game,\nincluding the error cases and the edge cases: empty input, duplicate column names, ragged rows,\nnulls, coercion failures, non-ASCII text and very large integers. So the spec text is the\ncontract, not your reading of what the toolkit ought to do - where the spec fixes an exact name,\nsignature, exception type, ordering or output string, match it exactly, and where it looks odd,\nimplement what it says.\n\nThere is more here than one agent can finish. The sections are separable on purpose; the\ndependencies that do cross a boundary are listed under \"Layout\" in SPEC.md.\n"},"at":"2026-09-03T10:14:10+00:00","by":"<operator>","event":"run_started","policy_version":"sha256:608f4367f7c41864ad2978e1193083b5ad45351389398c244385393a7b0b29b0","run_id":"2026